<section class="page-hero"><div class="ph-media"><img class="zoom" src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="680" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="shell ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><span aria-current="page">Appraisal process</span></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>The Tennessee Insurance Appraisal Process, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Eight stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where claims most often stall.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn outline-light" href="/get-a-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="shell layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Appraisal is a short paragraph in most Tennessee property policies, but it sets up a complete dispute-resolution process. Here is how it usually unfolds, step by step, and where claims tend to stall. Policy wording differs, so read your own form alongside this guide.</p>
<h2>Before you start: is this an amount dispute?</h2>
<p>Appraisal settles <strong>how much</strong> a covered loss costs. It isn’t designed to decide <strong>whether</strong> the loss is covered. If the carrier says “we’ll pay for six squares” and you say the roof needs twenty-eight, that’s an amount dispute. If the carrier says “wear and tear isn’t covered,” that’s a coverage dispute, and appraisal alone usually won’t solve it.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Get legal advice for denials</b>Tennessee law gives policyholders a statutory bad-faith remedy in certain circumstances, along with other contract remedies. Those are pursued through the courts, not through appraisal. For outright denials or approaching deadlines, consult a Tennessee attorney.</div>
<h2>The eight stages of a Tennessee appraisal</h2>
<ol class="steps"><li><h3>Pin down the disagreement</h3><p>Get the carrier’s full, itemized estimate in writing and compare it line by line with an independent estimate. Appraisal works only when the dispute is about the amount of a covered loss.</p></li><li><h3>Check the policy and the calendar</h3><p>Find the appraisal condition, the suit-limitation clause and any proof-of-loss request. Note every deadline before you act.</p></li><li><h3>Send a written demand</h3><p>Either side can demand appraisal in writing. Reference the policy and claim numbers, state that you disagree on the amount of loss, and name your appraiser or say you will within the policy’s window.</p></li><li><h3>Both sides name appraisers</h3><p>Each party selects a competent, impartial appraiser — commonly within 20 days of the demand. The carrier’s appraiser is often an independent firm it hires.</p></li><li><h3>Agree on an umpire</h3><p>The two appraisers select a neutral umpire. If they can’t agree within the policy’s window, either side can usually ask a court of record in the county where the property sits to appoint one.</p></li><li><h3>Inspect, price and negotiate</h3><p>The appraisers inspect, exchange estimates and work through disputed line items. Most of the gap usually narrows at this stage.</p></li><li><h3>Umpire review if needed</h3><p>Items still in dispute go to the umpire with photos, measurements and pricing support from each appraiser.</p></li><li><h3>Signed award and payment</h3><p>A written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. The carrier then pays under the policy’s terms, less the deductible and any prior payments.</p></li></ol>
<h2>What a well-prepared file contains</h2>
<ul>
<li>The complete policy, including declarations and endorsements.</li>
<li>The carrier’s itemized estimate and all correspondence, in date order.</li>
<li>An independent, line-item estimate — not a lump-sum bid.</li>
<li>Dated photos and video taken soon after the loss, plus current photos.</li>
<li>Mitigation invoices, drying logs and receipts for temporary repairs.</li>
<li>Any engineer, roofer or restoration reports.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tennessee-claim-tools/appraisal-readiness-check/">appraisal readiness check</a> scores your file in about two minutes.</p>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2>
<p>A simple residential appraisal often resolves within two to three months after both appraisers are named. Commercial losses, contents-heavy claims and panels that need a formal umpire hearing take longer. Delays usually come from missing documents, slow appraiser selection or disagreement over the umpire.</p>
<h2>What it costs</h2>
<p>Each side pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee is usually split evenly. Appraiser fees may be flat, hourly or a percentage of the increase. Use the <a href="/tennessee-claim-tools/claim-gap-calculator/">claim gap &amp; fee estimator</a> to see whether the math works before you start.</p>
<h2>After the award</h2>
<p>The award typically states replacement cost and actual cash value, and sometimes breaks the loss out by category. The carrier applies the deductible, subtracts prior payments and pays the balance. Recoverable depreciation follows the policy’s normal rules, usually after repairs are completed.</p>
</article>
